WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.043 --> 00:00:01.710
SHANE GLASS: Hi.

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:03.960
My name's Shane Glass, and
I'm the program manager

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.062
for Google Cloud's
public datasets program.

00:00:06.062 --> 00:00:08.520
And I'm here today to talk to
you about the public datasets

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.880
we have available in BigQuery
and Google Cloud Storage,

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.920
as well as how they can
support your workloads.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:17.870
And don't worry-- there's
a fun demo at the end, too.

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:19.910
Now, the mission of our
public dataset program

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:23.540
is quite simply to maximize the
availability and accessibility

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:25.910
of high-value, high-demand
public data assets

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:27.260
through Google Cloud.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.030
And we do this because
we fundamentally

00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:31.072
believe that it makes
life a little bit easier

00:00:31.072 --> 00:00:31.655
for our users.

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:37.140
We currently have more than 130
datasets in our public dataset

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:40.380
program, and these datasets
are onboarded and maintained

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:43.710
by Googlers with provider
input and guidance.

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:46.060
You can see here a sample
of our data providers,

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:50.040
including NOAA, the US Census
Bureau, the United Nations

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:52.110
Statistics Division,
the Federal Elections

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:53.280
Commission, and others.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:58.090
We're constantly
adding new datasets.

00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:01.390
And here are just three examples
of recently-added, unstructured

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:04.420
datasets, all coming from
NOAA-- the National Oceanic

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:06.160
and Atmospheric Administration.

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:10.690
They include a high-resolution
rapid refresh weather model,

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:14.920
the new GOES-16 satellite
imagery, and NOAA's National

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:16.690
Water Model that
provides stream flow

00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:21.440
information across the
continental United States.

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:25.100
We also have datasets in
BigQuery, including the GitHub

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:27.690
repository; the NOAA
Global Surface Summary

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:30.290
of the Day, that provides
meteorological observations

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:33.560
going back to 1900; and
the Ethereum blockchain,

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:37.430
along with six other
cryptocurrencies.

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:39.360
But don't just take
my word for it.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:41.240
Let's see a demo
specifically looking

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:44.060
at how you can use some
of our geospatial datasets

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:47.440
to support your workflows.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:49.560
So let's look at a
demonstration that tells you

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.102
how many lightning
strikes there were

00:01:51.102 --> 00:01:54.000
across the continental
United States in 2018,

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.060
thanks to the lightning data
we have available from NOAA.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:59.387
You can see here a query
that runs real quickly,

00:01:59.387 --> 00:02:01.470
and I'll give you a
concatenation and a little bit

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:02.887
cleaner format so
that it's easier

00:02:02.887 --> 00:02:05.280
to visualize this
data in Data Studio.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:07.110
So I'll click Run down
here, and a little

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:08.789
like they do in
the cooking shows,

00:02:08.789 --> 00:02:10.598
I already have it finished.

00:02:10.598 --> 00:02:12.640
You can see here the number
of lightning strikes,

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:14.610
with a center point
of a 1/10 degree

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:17.190
by 1/10 degree tile
that aggregates

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:19.020
the strikes across the US.

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:21.060
We'll take a look in
Data Studio and see

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:23.630
what we notice trend-wise.

00:02:23.630 --> 00:02:26.910
We'll flip over to our
cooking show finished version,

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:28.970
and you can see here
the aggregated number

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:32.850
of lightning strikes
across the year on average.

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:34.770
But this doesn't
really tell us much,

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:37.150
as it's hard to see the
specific data points.

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:41.070
So let's zoom in using the Zoom
Area function in Data Studio,

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:42.720
and we'll zoom in to
the United States.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.050
And this is primarily
where the data comes from.

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:47.950
Now, I had originally
expected Florida

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:49.770
to have the most
lightning strikes per day,

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:51.520
but as you can see,
what really stands out

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:54.950
on this map is the collection
of data here in Oklahoma.

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:57.422
Now I'm curious, and I
want to dig in further.

00:02:57.422 --> 00:02:59.380
And how can you use public
datasets to do that?

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:05.220
Now, we can use the geospatial
capabilities-- or GIS--

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:08.700
that are built into
BigQuery, and this

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:12.060
helps BigQuery understand
the geospatial relationship

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:13.590
between datasets.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:16.050
Now, this particular query
has a little pre-processing

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:17.970
done under the hood, but
we're hopeful to make

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:20.940
that unnecessary here
in the near future.

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:23.610
For now, we'll
take the shortcut.

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:25.650
And so this query
gives us the return

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:28.320
of a polygon for each
tile, as well as the number

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:29.995
of lightning strikes by day.

00:03:29.995 --> 00:03:31.620
But let's say we
wanted to look at that

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:33.520
in a more visual format.

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:36.450
Well, I'd come over to BigQuery
GeoViz, the geovisualization

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:39.480
capability available for free.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:43.510
I could come down here and run
the same query, see my results,

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:46.600
and define which
column is our geometry.

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:50.020
And from there, I give a
style that best fits our data.

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:52.420
In this case, I've given
an interval for the number

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:54.550
of strikes and location.

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:57.230
And I can tell you, as a
former Oklahoma resident,

00:03:57.230 --> 00:03:58.810
this makes a lot of sense to me.

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:02.020
The areas of Oklahoma that
are notorious for the storms

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:05.670
are also seeing the greatest
number of lightning strikes.

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:07.740
But this is pretty broad.

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:10.560
These are 1/10 of a degree
by 1/10 of a degree tiles

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:11.742
across the state.

00:04:11.742 --> 00:04:13.200
Well, what does
that actually mean?

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:16.839
No one measures the world
in 10th of degree tiles.

00:04:16.839 --> 00:04:19.029
Instead, we define the
world by boundaries--

00:04:19.029 --> 00:04:21.940
in most cases, the US political
and statistical boundaries,

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:24.910
such as either counties
or census tracts.

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:27.970
In this case, let's take a
look at one of the 10 boundary

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:30.010
datasets we have
available in BigQuery

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:31.930
through the public
dataset program

00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:34.703
that defines the space
around each of these borders.

00:04:34.703 --> 00:04:36.370
In this case, we'll
start with counties,

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:39.210
since that's a pretty
popular one in Oklahoma.

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:41.328
We run a pretty similar
query, except this time,

00:04:41.328 --> 00:04:43.120
we'll take a little
shortcut so that I only

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:45.620
have to remember the state name
as opposed to the state FIPS

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:47.100
code.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:48.970
And again, we'll
run our results,

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:53.390
and we'll see that counties this
time is the geometric column.

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:55.800
And again, we can
run our visualization

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:58.960
and add style that meets
the needs of our dataset.

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:02.220
And one of the nice features
about BigQuery GeoViz

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:05.400
is it allows me to go in and
click on an individual county

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:09.260
and see what the
exact outputs were.

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:12.240
And we can see
here, for example,

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:16.580
that Oklahoma County has about
300 lightning strikes per day.

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:18.330
And this pretty closely
lines up with what

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:21.120
we saw from the other map,
except on a more understandable

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:21.995
boundary.

00:05:21.995 --> 00:05:24.120
What if we want to look at
something more granular,

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:24.780
though--

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:28.170
say, for instance, ZIP codes?

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:29.730
Again, we'll take
the same dataset,

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:32.880
but this time we'll define it
by the spaces of ZIP codes--

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:35.990
or ZIP code tabulation
areas, in this case--

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:37.370
and we'll run that through.

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:39.140
And that gives you a
better idea of maybe

00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:41.210
what ZIP code you want
to avoid, assuming you

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:43.863
don't like lightning strikes.

00:05:43.863 --> 00:05:45.280
We'll go through
the same process,

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:47.590
and we'll define the same style.

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:51.770
This time, let's use a
different method of defining

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:53.690
that data-driven column.

00:05:53.690 --> 00:05:56.650
So let's use a
linear extrapolation.

00:05:56.650 --> 00:05:59.050
We'll give it a low of 27--

00:05:59.050 --> 00:06:01.070
as you can see, the
minimum down here--

00:06:01.070 --> 00:06:02.740
and then we'll go
up by 100 from here.

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:09.690
So 120, 220, 320, and so on.

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:18.260
And I'll want to get
over my max of 499.

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:19.902
I'll quickly set the
colors down here,

00:06:19.902 --> 00:06:21.860
and I can scroll through
some different options

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:23.750
to find one that
suits my palette.

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:25.250
In this case, I
want to do something

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:27.620
pretty similar to the one
I used in the counties.

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:29.390
And you can see
here again, there's

00:06:29.390 --> 00:06:32.330
a dark concentration here
around Oklahoma City.

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:35.330
But this time, I can drill
down to a more granular level

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:38.720
and see which ZIP codes might
make it the most likely for me

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:42.460
to see some lightning
strikes in my backyard.

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:44.170
So this is just one
example of what can

00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:45.778
be done with public datasets.

00:06:45.778 --> 00:06:48.070
If you're interested in
learning more, and figuring out

00:06:48.070 --> 00:06:51.310
how you can join public datasets
with your private datasets,

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:53.830
I encourage you to check
out GCP Marketplace

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:56.350
and click on Datasets
on the left-hand side.

00:06:56.350 --> 00:06:58.330
There, you can learn
more about datasets

00:06:58.330 --> 00:07:01.103
and get started
in BigQuery today.

